  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+1)}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x]^{\u2032}}{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+1)]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{(\\frac{1}{x+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u20610-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u20610}{(\\frac{1}{0+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
